        {"t": "h2", "text": "Concept block"},
        {"t": "b", "items": [
            "A microservice is a deployable with its own data and a business capability, owned by a team that can release it. A folder in a solution is a module. A database schema with a separate login is a step toward a boundary. A separate process with a shared database and shared tables is a distributed monolith.",
            "Start from a modular monolith when one team owns the domain and the boundaries are still moving. Extract a service when a part needs its own scale, its own release train, or a different availability target.",
            "Each service writes its own data. Another service gets that data through an API or a message, or it keeps its own copy that it accepts may lag.",
            "Synchronous HTTP is for a query the user is waiting on, when the caller can tolerate the dependency. Asynchronous messaging is for work that can finish after the response, and for fan-out.",
            "Azure Service Bus is for commands and business events that need locks, dead-letter, sessions, and a consumer. Event Grid is for event distribution and reacting to platform events. Event Hubs is a high-volume stream, closer to telemetry and ingestion than to one business command.",
            "The outbox pattern writes the business row and the message row in one transaction. A relay publishes the message and marks it sent. Consumers are idempotent.",
            "A saga is a long-running process with a forward path and a compensation path. Orchestration puts the policy in one process manager. Choreography lets each service react to events. Orchestration is easier to see and to change. Choreography couples services less and becomes harder to trace.",
            "Idempotency means doing the message twice has the same business result as doing it once. At-least-once delivery is the realistic Service Bus contract. Exactly-once processing is your deduplication, not a checkbox on the bus.",
            "Observability is logs, metrics, and traces with one correlation id crossing HTTP and the bus. A tech lead budgets for this in the first release.",
        ]},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "When would you split a service, and when would you keep a modular monolith?",
         "a": "I keep one deployable while one team is learning the domain, the transactions are local, and we can still draw modules with separate projects and separate schemas. I split when a capability has a different scaling profile, a different compliance boundary, or a team that must release without waiting for the rest. Orders and catalog often split because catalog reads dwarf order writes, or because different teams own them. I do not split because the diagram looks modern. Each split adds a network failure mode, a contract, a dashboard, and an on-call question. If I cannot name the data owner and the failure, the split is early.",
         "lead": "In the interview I draw modules first, then one dotted line where I would cut, and I say what has to be true before I cut: an independent schema, a contract test, and a team that can run the new service next week. That sequence sounds like a lead. A page of boxes on day one sounds like a diagram."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "Why is a database per service the hard part, and how do you query across them?",
         "a": "The hard part is the report that used to join Orders and Customers. After the split, that join is a distributed query. I handle it in one of three ways. The UI calls both services and composes, which fits a small screen. One service stores the few customer fields it needs, updated by events, which fits a hot path and accepts lag. A reporting store ingests both streams and serves the warehouse-style question, which fits analytics. I do not give the order service a SQL login on the customer tables. That shortcut recreates the coupling and hides it from the API review. Cross-service transactions that need both commits to succeed become a saga, not a TransactionScope over two databases.",
         "lead": "I say the reporting question out loud in design reviews, because it is the reason teams sneak shared databases back in. If I have an answer for the report, the boundary will hold."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "Explain the outbox. What fails if you publish after SaveChanges?",
         "a": "If I save the order and then publish, a crash or a bus outage between the two leaves a committed order and no message. The email never sends, and the inventory service never hears about it. If I publish and then save, a failed save leaves a message for an order that does not exist. The outbox inserts the order and an outbox row in one SQL transaction. A relay reads unpublished rows, sends them, and marks them published. The relay can send twice if it crashes after send and before the mark, so the consumer deduplicates on the message id. I poll the outbox or use a transaction log reader if the volume requires it. Polling is the version I can explain and operate.",
         "lead": "I would rather have a visible unpublished row I can alert on than a message that exists only in memory. The outbox table is an operational object. It has a count, an age, and an owner."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "Design a saga for order, payment, and inventory.",
         "a": "The order service accepts the command and writes an order in a Pending state. An orchestrator, which could be Durable Functions or a process manager in the order service, asks inventory to reserve, then asks payment to capture. If payment fails, it sends a release to inventory and marks the order Failed. If the release fails, the saga stays in a compensating state and alerts a human. Inventory and payment each enforce their own local transaction and their own idempotency key. There is no distributed lock across the three databases. Timeouts are explicit: a reservation expires if the saga never completes. I choose orchestration here because the compensation policy is a business rule I want in one place, testable, and visible to support. Choreography would hide that policy across three consumers.",
         "lead": "I name the stuck states. A lead design includes Pending too long, Reserve succeeded and Payment unknown, and Compensation failed. Those states need a screen or a query, not only a log line."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "What does idempotent consumption look like in SQL?",
         "a": "The message has a stable id. The consumer inserts that id into a ProcessedMessage table with a unique primary key in the same transaction as the business write. If the insert hits a duplicate key, this message was already applied, and the consumer completes it without doing the work again. For a create, the business unique key can be the idempotency key itself. For an increment, I store the message id so I do not add the quantity twice. I complete the bus message only after the transaction commits. If I complete first and then crash, the work is lost. If I commit first and then crash before complete, the message redelivers and the unique key saves me.",
         "lead": "I test this. A unit test that calls the handler twice is the cheapest proof. I have seen teams say the bus is exactly once and skip the unique key. One redelivery then double-shipped an order."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "How do you version an event?",
         "a": "I treat the event as a contract. I include a version, a message id, a correlation id, an occurred time, and a payload that is tolerant of new fields. Adding an optional field is a compatible change. Renaming or changing a meaning is a new event type or a new version, and I publish both until consumers move. I do not share a C# class library of entities as the contract across services, because a rebuild then couples their releases. A small contract package or a published schema is enough. Consumers ignore fields they do not understand. They do not throw on an unknown optional field and dead-letter the queue.",
         "lead": "A shared EntityFramework model project referenced by two services is a distributed monolith with extra steps. I say that clearly if they ask how teams share data models."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "What do you log, measure, and trace across services?",
         "a": "Every request and every message carries a correlation id, and if a message causes another message, a causation id. Logs are structured JSON: correlation id, service name, message id, tenant, and a duration. I do not log the full payload when it contains personal data. Metrics: request rate, latency, error rate, dependency latency, queue depth, dead-letter depth, outbox age, and saga state counts. Traces connect the HTTP call to SQL and to the consumer using OpenTelemetry. I alert on symptoms users feel and on queues that will become those symptoms soon. I keep one dashboard per service and one picture of the business flow.",
         "lead": "I budget tracing in the first milestone. Adding correlation ids after five services are in production is a project. A lead who ships the split without the dashboard has created a system the team cannot be on call for."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "A downstream service is down. What does the user see?",
         "a": "If the call was synchronous and required, the user sees a controlled 503 after the timeout and the circuit breaker, and we do not hold threads for a minute. If the work was asynchronous, the user sees 202 Accepted and a status they can poll, and the message waits on the bus or in the outbox. I decide that classification when we design the endpoint, not during the incident. I also decide the backlog behavior: if inventory is down, do we keep accepting orders into Pending, or do we stop at the edge? That is a business decision I record. Automatic retry with exponential backoff and a dead-letter path keeps the rest of the platform moving.",
         "lead": "I bring the product owner into the accept-versus-reject choice before go-live. Technology can implement either. The wrong default is an accident."},
